WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.620
Choosing an iteration pattern is an engineering decision, not a cosmetic decision about the canvas.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.840
The first option is normal item processing.

3
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:17.820
Use it when each incoming item can pass independently through ordinary nodes and the downstream operation already knows how to handle every item it receives.

4
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:28.160
A contact list moving through a message node, a row list moving through an update node, or a set of webhook events moving through a filter often fits this pattern.

5
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:32.360
The second option is Loop Over Items.

6
00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:43.500
Use it when the workflow needs to release items one at a time or in controlled batches, when a particular operation does not iterate the way the design requires, or when several nodes must act as one repeated body.

7
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:56.000
Loop Over Items is also useful when the body needs a deliberate pause, a diagnostic step, a branch, or a return connection that makes each iteration complete before the next batch is released.

8
00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.840
The third option is a native feature on a node.

9
00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:05.820
Some nodes have their own batching or pagination behavior, and the HTTP Request node is a common example.

10
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:16.480
If the node can request pages, limit items per batch, or wait between batches in a way that matches the external API, that may be simpler than drawing a manual loop.

11
00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:25.460
Native features are especially attractive when the repeated behavior is contained inside one node and does not need several workflow steps to preserve state.

12
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.820
The fourth option is a condition-controlled return connection.

13
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.040
This pattern is different from looping over a known list.

14
00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:37.220
It repeats until changing state says that the workflow should stop.

15
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:50.440
Pagination is the usual example: request a page, process the page, inspect whether another cursor, token, page number, or next-page URL exists, and either return for another request or exit.

16
00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:56.780
The loop is driven by state observed during execution, not only by the number of items that existed at the start.

17
00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:01.380
There is also a design boundary where a sub-workflow becomes a better choice.

18
00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:14.040
If the repeated logic contains many nodes, must be reused by several workflows, or has its own error handling and audit requirements, hiding it inside one large loop body can make maintenance painful.

19
00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:19.340
A sub-workflow gives that repeated process a name and a stable interface.

20
00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.480
It can make the main workflow easier to read while still allowing controlled repetition.

21
00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.000
Compare three common scenarios.

22
00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:38.540
Sending one Slack message for each existing contact normally belongs to ordinary item processing, because each contact is already an item and the message operation can run for each item.

23
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:51.520
Processing two thousand records in batches of twenty-five with a pause belongs either to native batching when a single node can handle it, or to Loop Over Items when an entire multi-node sequence needs pacing.

24
00:02:51.620 --> 00:03:05.860
Requesting successive API pages until a next-page token disappears belongs first to native HTTP Request pagination when it fits, and otherwise to a condition-controlled loop with explicit state and termination.

25
00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:11.660
A useful review habit is to name the unit of repetition before selecting the node pattern.

26
00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:18.080
The unit may be one contact, one batch, one page, or one reusable business operation.

27
00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.660
If the team cannot name that unit clearly, the loop design is probably premature.

28
00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:31.720
Naming the unit also makes it easier to place logs, retries, waits, and done-path summaries in the correct part of the workflow.

29
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.760
The tradeoff is that explicit loops add responsibilities.

30
00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:45.940
A visible loop has execution state, a loop path, a done path, a return connection, and a way to fail by repeating too often or by stopping too early.

31
00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:49.160
Use that structure when it solves a real requirement.

32
00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:53.900
Avoid it when it only makes the workflow resemble procedural code.

33
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.800
The most reliable n8n workflows use the simplest iteration pattern that accurately expresses the work.
